  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date the Department of Justice has obligated $6.6B on federal contracts across 39,156 awards, with an average award size of $169K. Obligations are down 22.5% year over year. Annual totals illustrate a downward move from a peak: FY23 was $10B, then FY24 $9.4B, FY25 $8.5B, and FY26 $6.6B so far. The largest spending categories in FY2026 to date include Other Computer Related Services at $771M and Security Guards and Patrol Services at $758M, followed by General Medical and Surgical Hospitals at $593M and Other Residential Care Facilities at $441M. The top vendor by obligations is METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. with $517M, followed by NAPHCARE LLC at $170M and AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC at $149M. Other notable vendors include THE GEO GROUP, INC. and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $145M each, and CGI FEDERAL INC. at $138M. The portfolio shows significant spending in IT services, medical and residential care, security, and legal and management consulting services.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have DOJ contract obligations trended recently?",
        "body": "Obligations are down 22.5% year over year. Annual obligations moved from $10B in FY23 to $9.4B in FY24, $8.5B in FY25, and $6.6B in FY26 to date. That pattern shows lower annual spending in the current fiscal year to date compared with prior years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the big spending categories and who wins them?",
        "body": "IT and security services lead the FY2026 to date list, with Other Computer Related Services at $771M and Security Guards and Patrol Services at $758M. Medical and residential care are also large, with General Medical and Surgical Hospitals at $593M and Other Residential Care Facilities at $441M. METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. is the top vendor at $517M, with several healthcare and IT contractors also among the top ten."
      }
    ]
  },
